3. GATEWAY IMAGE DEPENDENCIES (Node / npm)
================================================================================

These arrive through the OpenClaw workspace, not through Rem's own code. They
are relevant because a workspace-wide install pulls every extension's
`node_modules` into the gateway image whether or not that extension is enabled.
The image itself is built and operated separately (private); see "CONVEYANCE".

Across the two lockfiles in this repository — 1,453 packages resolved from the
OpenClaw workspace and 284 from `backend/` — there is no GPL-only, AGPL, or SSPL
package. The only GPL-family entries are the
LGPL items in §3.2 and one dual-licensed package where MIT is electable (§3.4).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3.1 @anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk — NOT AN OSI LICENSE. Read this one.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  @anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk @ 0.2.132   ->  "SEE LICENSE IN README.md"

Its eight platform binary subpackages at the same version (`-darwin-arm64`,
`-darwin-x64`, `-linux-arm64`, `-linux-arm64-musl`, `-linux-x64`,
`-linux-x64-musl`, `-win32-arm64`, `-win32-x64`) declare
"SEE LICENSE IN LICENSE.md".

These are Anthropic's own commercial terms, not an open-source license. It
reaches us transitively: `extensions/acpx` -> `@agentclientprotocol/claude-agent-acp@0.33.1`
-> `@anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk`.

There is no copyleft here and nothing infects Rem's own code, which remains
Apache-2.0. But the consequence is worth stating plainly:

  *** THE BUILT GATEWAY IMAGE IS NOT REDISTRIBUTABLE UNDER AN OSI LICENSE. ***

Anyone who forks this repository and builds the gateway image is bound by
Anthropic's terms for that component, not by ours. This is the single most
likely thing to surprise a downstream user.

